00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:35.926
So, when you are creating
visualizations, what we have to

00:05:35.927 --> 00:05:39.556
do is we have to create a visual
representation of abstract data.

00:05:39.557 --> 00:05:41.932
And this helps us to
amplify cognition.

00:05:41.933 --> 00:05:44.752
The main task of
the visualization designer is

00:05:44.753 --> 00:05:48.368
then to define how abstract
data should be encoded visually

00:05:48.369 --> 00:05:51.639
without having to worry
about little of the details.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.870
So to scales, or axes,
or how to draw these.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:00.390
Take for example, this weather
data, with information

00:06:00.391 --> 00:06:05.400
about the precipitation, and
wind temperature in Seattle.

00:06:05.401 --> 00:06:08.790
This data doesn't have an
inherent visual representation.

00:06:08.791 --> 00:06:11.730
Here I create the table
to show you the data,

00:06:11.731 --> 00:06:16.650
but inherently this data doesn't
have a visual representation.

00:06:16.651 --> 00:06:18.660
So if the analysts decides
that they want to look at

00:06:18.661 --> 00:06:20.640
a particular aspect,
they can for

00:06:20.641 --> 00:06:23.680
example create a strip
lot of the temperature.

00:06:23.681 --> 00:06:28.994
And this shows us what they
temperature range is in Seattle.

00:06:28.995 --> 00:06:32.449
In a bigger light a single
view like this one here is

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:36.319
specified by encoding
properties of as

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.460
explained earlier in the based
on the grammar of graphics.

00:06:40.461 --> 00:06:44.176
So Strip plot is a visualization
where we encode the value as

00:06:44.177 --> 00:06:46.268
the exposition of the tick mark.

00:06:47.534 --> 00:06:54.966
Temperature here is variable
that shows a magnitude So now,

00:06:54.967 --> 00:06:58.643
our syntax, what we need to do
is we need to specify the data

00:06:58.644 --> 00:07:02.560
source, and then, the mark that
should represent the data.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.740
And most importantly,
we have to define the mapping

00:07:06.741 --> 00:07:10.730
of data values to visual
properties of that mark.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:13.520
And in our language,

00:07:13.521 --> 00:07:17.290
we express all of this in
a declarative JS range.

00:07:17.291 --> 00:07:20.120
And JSON is very easy to
process with common programming

00:07:20.121 --> 00:07:22.800
languages and it's native to the
web which is why we picked it.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.160
Behind the scenes,
the Vega-Lite compiler

00:07:27.161 --> 00:07:31.230
reasons about the visualization
and results ambiguity about low

00:07:31.231 --> 00:07:34.330
level details such as
the scales and axes.

00:07:34.331 --> 00:07:37.420
And it does so with reasonable
defaults as you can see here.

00:07:37.421 --> 00:07:40.398
And emitting these details
allows us to have a more concise

00:07:40.399 --> 00:07:41.320
specification.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.456
These defaults in Vega-Lite
are designed to follow best

00:07:45.457 --> 00:07:47.308
practices in
visualization design.

00:07:50.015 --> 00:07:53.540
But if the user wants to
customize it, they can override.

00:07:53.541 --> 00:07:55.240
So any of these properties
that you see here

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:56.150
you can override, for example.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.850
So going back to the strip plot,
it shows us the spread

00:08:00.851 --> 00:08:03.490
of the temperature in Seattle,
which ranges

00:08:03.491 --> 00:08:07.250
from a couple negative degrees
to around 36 degrees Celsius.

00:08:07.251 --> 00:08:10.727
However, it doesn't show as how
many days have a particular

00:08:10.728 --> 00:08:11.619
temperature.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:14.696
So we don't know whether
it's one or 10 or

00:08:14.697 --> 00:08:18.035
more days that have this
15 degrees Celsius.

00:08:19.969 --> 00:08:23.102
And in order to see that, we
could create a Histogram, which

00:08:23.103 --> 00:08:27.010
shows the number of records that
fall into particular ranges.

00:08:27.011 --> 00:08:30.350
In other words, a histogram is a
visualization of a distribution.

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:33.405
So now the question's, how do
we create this in Vega-Lite.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:36.000
Well, in the grammar
of graphics,

00:08:36.001 --> 00:08:41.150
a histogram is a bar chart with
the bars the underlying mark.

00:08:41.151 --> 00:08:45.230
The exposition is determined
by a binned field and

00:08:45.231 --> 00:08:48.470
the aggregated count maps
to the length of the bars.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:54.580
So let's go back to the strip
lot that we had earlier and

00:08:54.581 --> 00:08:56.680
transition to the histogram.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.670
First, we discretize
the temperature data.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:06.110
Then we encode the count, so

00:09:06.111 --> 00:09:08.900
an aggregate, as the y
position of the tick mark.

00:09:08.901 --> 00:09:11.570
Notice it already looks
kind of like the histogram.

00:09:11.571 --> 00:09:15.840
All we have to do is change
the mark type to bar, and

00:09:15.841 --> 00:09:17.830
now we have a histogram.

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.280
And yeah, so this is how
you create a histogram for

00:09:23.281 --> 00:09:24.600
quantitative data in Vega-Lite.

00:09:24.601 --> 00:09:26.880
You can change the data to a bit
different data settings, and

00:09:26.881 --> 00:09:28.340
it will still work the same way.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:31.805
To make sense of histograms,

00:09:31.806 --> 00:09:34.880
Vega-Lite uses a binning
algorithm, which discretizes

00:09:34.881 --> 00:09:38.198
the quantitive data in human
understandable intervals.

00:09:38.199 --> 00:09:42.710
Beyond that, Vega-Lite also
determines sensible defaults

00:09:42.711 --> 00:09:44.430
to parametrize the binning.

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:49.260
In the histogram,
we bin the x-axis,

00:09:49.261 --> 00:09:51.070
which is the positional channel.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:54.005
Instead of showing
an ordinal access,

00:09:54.006 --> 00:09:57.230
Vega-Lite actually creates
a quantitative axis as a guide.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.810
We know from the visualization
data chart dating back

00:10:00.811 --> 00:10:01.330
to Cleveland and

00:10:01.331 --> 00:10:05.590
McGill that the position is
also highly discriminable.

00:10:05.591 --> 00:10:09.510
So we can use a large number of
bins to show the temperature.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:13.590
If you instead use
a non-positional channel,

00:10:13.591 --> 00:10:15.050
such as color, opacity, or

00:10:15.051 --> 00:10:19.370
shape, then Vega-Lite generates
a legend with range labels.

00:10:19.371 --> 00:10:22.690
So rather than
the axis as the guide.

00:10:22.691 --> 00:10:25.280
And it also uses
fewer bins because

00:10:25.281 --> 00:10:27.550
color is less discriminable.

00:10:27.551 --> 00:10:29.590
We have a harder time telling
apart different colors.

00:10:29.591 --> 00:10:32.414
It's much easier for us to tell
apart different positions.

00:10:34.622 --> 00:10:38.184
So having a grammar allows us to
reason about the specification,

00:10:38.185 --> 00:10:40.890
and optimize the visual output.

00:10:40.891 --> 00:10:41.580
With the grammar,

00:10:41.581 --> 00:10:44.290
we can incrementally customize
the chart as I've shown you.

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:48.350
Another incremental
customization is to add

00:10:48.351 --> 00:10:50.260
an encoding to
an existing visualization.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:52.440
So for instance,

00:10:52.441 --> 00:10:57.430
we can encode the dominant
weather type using the color.

00:10:57.431 --> 00:11:00.150
And then we get histogram that
shows us what the dominant

00:11:00.151 --> 00:11:05.478
weather this is for days with
the particular temperature.

00:11:05.479 --> 00:11:10.680
By default,
Vega-Lite colors that

00:11:10.681 --> 00:11:14.310
don't resonate with my feelings
are about these weather types.

00:11:14.311 --> 00:11:19.649
So we can customize the scale,
domain and range so that this

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:24.190
that are drizzlers more gray,
just hard feel with drizzle.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.460
So when we edit the color
encoding, Vega-Lite

00:11:29.461 --> 00:11:32.610
automatically stacked the bars
instead of layering them.

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:38.180
This is another example of
these sensible defaults.

00:11:38.181 --> 00:11:40.408
Because of the stacking,

00:11:40.409 --> 00:11:44.670
the overall shape of
the histogram is preserved.

00:11:44.671 --> 00:11:46.500
And this is what you can
see here on the right.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:51.460
In this case,
multiple things are interacting.

00:11:51.461 --> 00:11:54.410
To do this kind of reasoning,
the Vega-Lite compiler took into

00:11:54.411 --> 00:11:58.710
account the channel, which was
color, the mark type, which was

00:11:58.711 --> 00:12:03.290
bar, and a layout transform,
which is the stacking.

00:12:03.291 --> 00:12:06.840
And these three components
interacted in a non-trivial way

00:12:06.841 --> 00:12:09.520
to create a stacked chart which
is more expressive than the bar

00:12:09.521 --> 00:12:10.770
chart with overlap.

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:14.540
Just to show you that this
overlap happens if I change this

00:12:14.541 --> 00:12:17.830
to a line you can see that

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.960
these bars are in fact
on top of each other.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.170
And so snow would be completely
hidden behind these other bars.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.180
The stacked histogram more
accurately shows us the data

00:12:31.181 --> 00:12:33.280
than a e-version would.

00:12:33.281 --> 00:12:38.420
It's still hard to compare
particular bars here.

00:12:38.421 --> 00:12:40.420
For example,
these two gray boxes here,

00:12:40.421 --> 00:12:43.700
because they don't share
a common baseline.

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:49.900
So instead, we could take this
histogram with color, instead of

00:12:49.901 --> 00:12:54.870
using color, we could lay out
all of these bars in space.

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:59.350
And so now we create one
histogram for each weather type.

00:12:59.351 --> 00:13:03.230
And it makes easier to
compare the boxes here.

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:08.040
And this is the first
example of Layered and

00:13:08.041 --> 00:13:10.415
Multi-view composition
in Vega-Lite.

00:13:10.416 --> 00:13:14.600
Vega-Lite has the grammar

00:13:14.601 --> 00:13:18.640
to compose views and makes
them fit to new opportunities.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:23.800
And in our grammar, we've
developed four basic operators.

00:13:23.801 --> 00:13:26.650
The first one you've
already seen, the faceting,

00:13:26.651 --> 00:13:29.260
which we just used to lay
out the histograms in space.

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:33.440
And what it does is it
partitions the data by an

00:13:33.441 --> 00:13:36.920
ordinal field and then creates a
view for each of the partitions.

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:43.900
A facet is the only operator
where each view gets

00:13:43.901 --> 00:13:46.270
a subset of the data.

00:13:46.271 --> 00:13:47.400
In the other operators,

00:13:47.401 --> 00:13:49.500
each subview gets access
to the full data.

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:53.530
One of those other ones
is the layer and operator

00:13:53.531 --> 00:13:56.030
where we can stack multiple
views on top of each other.

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:01.182
With the concatenation, we can
combine arbitrary views and

00:14:01.183 --> 00:14:03.437
create complex
multi-view displaced.

00:14:05.773 --> 00:14:10.339
And repeat is a concise and data
driven way to concatenate views.

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:14.500
And as a running example
to go into details for

00:14:14.501 --> 00:14:17.810
this, for operators,
we'll use this histogram here

00:14:17.811 --> 00:14:21.864
that shows the monthly
precipitation in Seattle.

00:14:21.865 --> 00:14:25.301
As might not see this beautiful
place but we try to scare

00:14:25.302 --> 00:14:29.264
tourists by claiming its raining
all the time which is of course

00:14:29.265 --> 00:14:32.498
not true unless you're in
November and December.

00:14:32.499 --> 00:14:36.190
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which we are right now.

00:14:36.191 --> 00:14:37.973
Anyway, let's talk
about the square chart.

00:14:39.924 --> 00:14:41.342
So in this chart,

00:14:41.343 --> 00:14:46.068
the x-axis encodes the date
which is grouped by month, and

00:14:46.069 --> 00:14:50.888
here we use this field time unit
which automatically puts all

00:14:50.889 --> 00:14:54.210
the days with the same
month into one bin.

00:14:55.906 --> 00:14:59.491
The length of the bar
encodes the mean or

00:14:59.492 --> 00:15:05.367
average precipitation of all the
days that fall into the month.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:09.792
So with the layer operator,

00:15:09.793 --> 00:15:12.733
we can plot multiple views
on top of each other and

00:15:12.734 --> 00:15:15.990
this can be useful for
example to annotate the chart.

00:15:15.991 --> 00:15:19.870
To use it, we first nest
the specification under

00:15:19.871 --> 00:15:20.830
the layer keyword.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:24.240
And then we can add
another view or

00:15:24.241 --> 00:15:27.290
a second specification
to this list of use.

00:15:27.291 --> 00:15:31.090
And now the layer can show
the mean precipitation

00:15:31.091 --> 00:15:31.970
with the rule mark.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:34.300
And note that in this case here,

00:15:34.301 --> 00:15:38.040
we only specify the y
location of this rule mark.

00:15:38.041 --> 00:15:40.200
So that we get
an overall average for

00:15:40.201 --> 00:15:41.710
the whole year,
not broken down by month.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:46.747
And Vega-Lite automatically
makes the two layers share the x

00:15:46.748 --> 00:15:48.160
and y-axis.

00:15:48.161 --> 00:15:51.727
In this case,
only the y-axis and

00:15:51.728 --> 00:15:56.160
the rule spans the full
width of the chart.

00:15:56.161 --> 00:15:59.222
And this is an example of this
kind of reasoning that goes on

00:15:59.223 --> 00:16:01.102
behind the scenes
in the compiler.

00:16:03.591 --> 00:16:05.420
Another operator is the concat.

00:16:05.421 --> 00:16:08.714
And we can use it
to a single few,

00:16:08.715 --> 00:16:13.350
like the histogram we
had at the beginning and

00:16:13.351 --> 00:16:17.865
visually concatenated
with another view.

00:16:17.866 --> 00:16:21.699
In Vega-Lite, you invoke this
by just repping multiple single

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:23.829
view specifications
in an array and

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:26.790
then nest them under
the concat keyword.

00:16:26.791 --> 00:16:29.830
Here we have the vconcat which
is for vertical concatenation.

00:16:29.831 --> 00:16:32.219
We also have hconcat for
horizontal concatenation.

00:16:34.506 --> 00:16:38.586
And the recurring design pattern
that we identified in these

00:16:38.587 --> 00:16:41.866
concatenated views is that
we often have the same

00:16:41.867 --> 00:16:43.067
specification.

00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:45.561
Where just the field
is different.

00:16:45.562 --> 00:16:48.849
Like in this case here, the only
thing that changed is whether

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:52.024
there's precipitation or
temperature on the y axis here.

00:16:53.487 --> 00:16:57.037
And because this is such a
common operation, we promoted it

00:16:57.038 --> 00:17:00.310
to it's own operator, and
we call this, The Repeat.

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:06.726
To use it, we start with a
specification of a single view,

00:17:06.727 --> 00:17:11.805
and then nest the specification
and add some parameters.

00:17:11.806 --> 00:17:15.439
First, we specify the list of
fields that we want to repeat

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:18.197
over or that we're
going create views for.

00:17:20.875 --> 00:17:25.115
And then we replace
the fields name with

00:17:25.116 --> 00:17:28.978
a reference to that
repeated data.

00:17:28.979 --> 00:17:33.543
And so now we create one chart
for precipitation, one chart for

00:17:33.544 --> 00:17:36.928
the temperature, and
one chart for the wind.

00:17:36.929 --> 00:17:38.576
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:38.577 --> 00:17:39.271
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:17:39.272 --> 00:17:40.528
&gt;&gt; Did you change
the aggregate search?

00:17:40.529 --> 00:17:41.928
&gt;&gt; Not right now.

00:17:41.929 --> 00:17:42.673
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:17:42.674 --> 00:17:45.265
&gt;&gt; But
that's something [INAUDIBLE],

00:17:45.266 --> 00:17:49.555
right now we only [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Keyword

00:17:49.556 --> 00:17:51.134
[INAUDIBLE].

00:17:51.135 --> 00:17:55.101
&gt;&gt; Yeah, here it's a keyword
because [INAUDIBLE] inside into

00:17:55.102 --> 00:17:56.060
a column.

00:17:56.061 --> 00:18:00.150
If you call it row, it lays out
in a row, and we can actually

00:18:00.151 --> 00:18:03.500
take this a step further and
do both at the same time.

00:18:03.501 --> 00:18:06.430
And this lets us
create a view for

00:18:06.431 --> 00:18:09.200
each entry in the cross-product
of these two lists.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.897
And so that's why we
can create this chart,

00:18:13.898 --> 00:18:17.886
which is scattered plot matrix,
or SPLOM for short.

00:18:20.045 --> 00:18:23.039
Of course, you could repeat
arbitrary views, but

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.300
here was a scatter plot.

00:18:25.301 --> 00:18:28.620
And these SPLOMS are very useful
to investigate relationship

00:18:28.621 --> 00:18:31.260
between multiple
quantity of variables.

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:33.814
And those can be, for
example [INAUDIBLE]-

00:18:33.815 --> 00:18:35.461
&gt;&gt; A lot of the times you see

00:18:35.462 --> 00:18:38.390
histograms for
those diagonal lines.

00:18:38.391 --> 00:18:41.356
Is there a way to do
that in this, or-

00:18:41.357 --> 00:18:42.197
&gt;&gt; You would have to do

00:18:42.198 --> 00:18:43.517
concatenation in that case.

00:18:46.546 --> 00:18:50.650
Yeah, so you can use these
SPLOMs as part of a dashboard.

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:56.400
And some of them may even
be composed in a dashboard.

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:00.590
So as I said, a dashboard
can have multiple views.

00:19:00.591 --> 00:19:04.270
And they can be quite useful to
make sense of larger amounts

00:19:04.271 --> 00:19:06.780
of data and
the relationships between them.

00:19:06.781 --> 00:19:08.230
However, when you create
these dashboards,

00:19:08.231 --> 00:19:10.440
you could of course
create them in Vega-Lite.

00:19:10.441 --> 00:19:12.904
If you create every
view separately,

00:19:12.905 --> 00:19:16.908
we would have to manually reason
about the data management and

00:19:16.909 --> 00:19:20.684
how these charts fit together,
or how they're laid out.

00:19:23.597 --> 00:19:26.913
And this is where the grammar
aspect of composition comes

00:19:26.914 --> 00:19:28.060
into play.

00:19:28.061 --> 00:19:28.870
And to illustrate this,

00:19:28.871 --> 00:19:32.450
let's dissect this dashboard
that you see on the left.

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:36.280
So first,
we have a bunch of single views.

00:19:36.281 --> 00:19:39.410
We have the histogram with
the different colored bursts and

00:19:39.411 --> 00:19:41.640
a scatter plot and a bar chart.

00:19:41.641 --> 00:19:46.770
A word histogram and the average
line and then what we do is

00:19:46.771 --> 00:19:50.570
we can use the facet, repeat and
layer to live in modern space,

00:19:50.571 --> 00:19:54.530
combine or annotate them
into these composed views.

00:19:56.190 --> 00:19:58.770
The cool thing is in Vega Lite,
we can actually recombine

00:19:58.771 --> 00:20:03.210
these composed views again
using the four basic operators.

00:20:03.211 --> 00:20:03.920
So for instance,

00:20:03.921 --> 00:20:07.260
we can take the three-layered
views on the right, and

00:20:07.261 --> 00:20:10.140
create them in a data driven
way by repeating a layer.

00:20:11.470 --> 00:20:14.010
But this hierarchical
composition that's going on here

00:20:14.011 --> 00:20:15.530
can continue.

00:20:15.531 --> 00:20:20.426
So we can combine the repeat
the two repeated views within

00:20:20.427 --> 00:20:24.710
horizontally concatenation and
then vertically

00:20:24.711 --> 00:20:29.610
concatenate that with the
[INAUDIBLE] view on the bottom.

00:20:29.611 --> 00:20:34.940
&gt;&gt; Maybe I'm stuck, what's the
difference between [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:34.941 --> 00:20:39.276
&gt;&gt; Facet is you define an
ordinal field that you partition

00:20:39.277 --> 00:20:43.612
the data by, and repeat is
just repeat the same view and

00:20:43.613 --> 00:20:46.832
every view gets access
to the full data.

00:20:46.833 --> 00:20:47.937
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:20:47.938 --> 00:20:48.437
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:54.913
&gt;&gt; So when we have this facet
down here, every view is for

00:20:54.914 --> 00:20:56.174
each web type.

00:20:56.175 --> 00:20:58.913
Okay, I understand
your confusion

00:20:58.914 --> 00:21:02.240
here because they
are very similar.

00:21:02.241 --> 00:21:04.225
You can think of it-
&gt;&gt; One is logically horizontal,

00:21:04.226 --> 00:21:04.984
one is logically vertical.

00:21:04.985 --> 00:21:06.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah, one is vertical,
one is horizontal.

00:21:06.361 --> 00:21:07.880
So if you could
transpose the data,

00:21:07.881 --> 00:21:09.180
you could use one operator.

00:21:09.181 --> 00:21:10.400
Here we have two because

00:21:10.401 --> 00:21:12.260
sometimes data just has
a particular format.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:16.410
Okay, so oops.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:23.120
So we created this whole view
with a single specification.

00:21:23.121 --> 00:21:26.901
And because of this declarative,
Vega-Lite can actually optimize

00:21:26.902 --> 00:21:30.300
the underlying data flow that's
below this visualization.

00:21:30.301 --> 00:21:34.682
And in Vega-Lite, then the JSON
format, this hierarchical

00:21:34.683 --> 00:21:38.232
composition is expressed
as you would expect it.

00:21:38.233 --> 00:21:42.014
You can use an [INAUDIBLE]
specification in place of any

00:21:42.015 --> 00:21:44.120
single view specification.

00:21:45.215 --> 00:21:48.420
Vega-Lite and automatically
reasons about the data

00:21:48.421 --> 00:21:52.119
management, and how scales and
axes should be combined.

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:56.560
And these efforts
are a powerful way to look

00:21:56.561 --> 00:21:57.690
at multidimensional data.

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:02.340
However, to really interrogate
the data further you want to

00:22:02.341 --> 00:22:06.230
interact with linked views and
then cover hidden relationships.

00:22:06.231 --> 00:22:10.100
And let me show you how
to do that in Vega-Lite.

00:22:10.101 --> 00:22:12.470
And this is where
the interaction part comes in.

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:19.500
So the basic building block for
our grammar of interactions

00:22:19.501 --> 00:22:23.150
are these selections, and they
are the core building blocks.

00:22:23.151 --> 00:22:26.991
Every selection defines
three components.

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:32.544
First, it is the event
processing, or how points that

00:22:32.545 --> 00:22:36.811
are in the selection are chosen
interactively, for example,

00:22:36.812 --> 00:22:39.968
whether it should happen
on a click or a hover.

00:22:39.969 --> 00:22:44.940
Then the second thing is
the points of interest which

00:22:44.941 --> 00:22:49.928
might be the data [INAUDIBLE] or
the visual elements.

00:22:49.929 --> 00:22:53.395
And then lastly, a predicate
function to determine if a point

00:22:53.396 --> 00:22:55.063
lies within this selection or

00:22:55.064 --> 00:22:58.545
not Let's make these selections
a little bit more concrete.

00:22:58.546 --> 00:23:03.208
Let's star with a simple
unit specification that

00:23:03.209 --> 00:23:06.870
shows a scatterplot for cars.

00:23:06.871 --> 00:23:10.369
So here the horsepower is on x,
the miles per gallon on y, and

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:13.612
the points are colored by
their manufacturing origin.

00:23:16.258 --> 00:23:21.239
The simplest definition of the
selection consists of a name,

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:24.606
here, it's called picked,
and a type.

00:23:24.607 --> 00:23:28.520
And then we can use some
conditional logic to change

00:23:28.521 --> 00:23:32.440
the color of the points
based on what is clicked on.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:36.440
So how’s this working?

00:23:36.441 --> 00:23:39.840
The selection type is driving a
lot the default values here for

00:23:39.841 --> 00:23:41.090
the selection.

00:23:41.091 --> 00:23:44.070
So the three components that I
described earlier they are all

00:23:44.071 --> 00:23:48.090
here but
of them are in the defaults.

00:23:48.091 --> 00:23:50.830
So in particular this
single selection here

00:23:50.831 --> 00:23:52.360
is saying unclick.

00:23:52.361 --> 00:23:54.364
Select a single point,
not on hover.

00:23:58.563 --> 00:24:01.731
And so, yeah,
when you click them,

00:24:01.732 --> 00:24:04.601
they get added to the selection.

00:24:06.806 --> 00:24:10.332
We can change the type
of the selection so

00:24:10.333 --> 00:24:14.400
that we can add
multiple points to it.

00:24:14.401 --> 00:24:18.010
Now the default says, on the
first click select the point.

00:24:18.011 --> 00:24:21.290
And with Shift click,
we can toggle points in and

00:24:21.291 --> 00:24:22.814
out of the selection.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:28.714
And like with encodings,

00:24:28.715 --> 00:24:33.054
we can override these defaults.

00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:35.360
So for example, we can do,

00:24:35.361 --> 00:24:38.660
instead of unclick,
we can do it on mouse hover.

00:24:38.661 --> 00:24:43.027
And now we get this kind
of brushing effect here.

00:24:45.876 --> 00:24:48.560
Okay, so besides the selections,

00:24:48.561 --> 00:24:52.140
we also introduced
selection transforms, and

00:24:52.141 --> 00:24:55.997
they're analogous to
the data transformations.

00:24:55.998 --> 00:25:00.915
They're a way to manipulate what
it means to be in a selection.

00:25:02.968 --> 00:25:06.104
So let me show you
how these work.

00:25:06.105 --> 00:25:09.575
Here is the single view
specification from earlier.

00:25:09.576 --> 00:25:13.991
And as a data analyst, we might
have a question of how does

00:25:13.992 --> 00:25:18.131
the number of cylinders of
a car has to play into a trend

00:25:18.132 --> 00:25:21.812
that we're seeing here
between horsepower and

00:25:21.813 --> 00:25:25.140
[INAUDIBLE] kind of
negative correlation.

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:30.990
And so to look at that,
we can use the selection

00:25:33.130 --> 00:25:37.760
and add a projection transform.

00:25:38.920 --> 00:25:44.060
And this allows us to limit
the selection to one field

00:25:44.061 --> 00:25:45.620
of the data.

00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:50.900
So now, when we’re clicking
a particular point,

00:25:50.901 --> 00:25:53.980
all the points that have
the same number of cylinders

00:25:53.981 --> 00:25:55.020
are highlighted as well.

00:25:55.021 --> 00:25:59.352
And with this interaction, I’m
able to see that there’s kind

00:25:59.353 --> 00:26:03.521
of three groups of cylinders,
that’s eight at the bottom,

00:26:03.522 --> 00:26:06.563
six at the mid-range,
and four at the top.

00:26:06.564 --> 00:26:10.469
But to sort of direct
manipulation by clicking doesn't

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:14.625
really give me a sense of how
exhausted I'm looking at these

00:26:14.626 --> 00:26:15.303
points.

00:26:15.304 --> 00:26:18.959
So they maybe, I don't know,
maybe cards were like

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:23.278
25 all this and I wouldn't even
be able to see them unless I,

00:26:23.279 --> 00:26:25.375
supposedly, click on them.

00:26:25.376 --> 00:26:28.834
So besides this sort of
direct manipulation,

00:26:28.835 --> 00:26:31.583
we can invoke a transform and
add some

00:26:31.584 --> 00:26:36.308
dynamic query widgets to the
interactions that we've added.

00:26:36.309 --> 00:26:41.325
And now [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:41.326 --> 00:26:46.685
So now, we're binding
this to an input element.

00:26:46.686 --> 00:26:50.999
And I can do the same clicking
and interaction in this chart

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:55.135
above, but we can also move
the slider back and forth and

00:26:55.136 --> 00:26:59.620
see that there's cars with
three and five cylinders.

00:26:59.621 --> 00:27:02.507
But only very few are with
three cylinders, so

00:27:02.508 --> 00:27:06.365
it's kind of hard to click on
them if you're just using this.

00:27:11.065 --> 00:27:15.068
And because Vega-Lite is
a declarative specification,

00:27:15.069 --> 00:27:18.744
where all the defaults get
filled in automatically,

00:27:18.745 --> 00:27:22.992
it's very easy to make small
changes and rapidly generate and

00:27:22.993 --> 00:27:25.622
evaluate hypotheses
over this data.

00:27:25.623 --> 00:27:27.882
&gt;&gt; Question?

00:27:27.883 --> 00:27:29.082
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:27:29.083 --> 00:27:32.034
&gt;&gt; So you're showing
now introduction of

00:27:32.035 --> 00:27:33.470
some UI controls.

00:27:33.471 --> 00:27:35.360
Is there any support for
theming?

00:27:35.361 --> 00:27:38.482
And I can image getting this
visualization into a larger

00:27:38.483 --> 00:27:42.071
application, and then wanting
to have those controls look like

00:27:42.072 --> 00:27:45.008
the other elements of
control in that application.

00:27:45.009 --> 00:27:47.418
&gt;&gt; So here, these
are the standard HTML elements.

00:27:47.419 --> 00:27:54.083
This is just a standard
range [CROSSTALK].

00:27:56.425 --> 00:28:00.818
Okay, so here in this example,
I've edit just a few more

00:28:00.819 --> 00:28:05.123
lines of additional codes so
that we can look an addition

00:28:05.124 --> 00:28:08.696
to just the effect of
the number of cylinders,

00:28:08.697 --> 00:28:13.390
also, what the effect of year
is on this, so now we have two.

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:16.730
And so perhaps luckily for

00:28:16.731 --> 00:28:21.360
us, we can see that as we're
scrubbing on the year, that if

00:28:21.361 --> 00:28:25.550
we're going forward in time cars
are becoming more efficient.

00:28:25.551 --> 00:28:28.443
The timelines are moving
up the scalar plot if we're

00:28:28.444 --> 00:28:30.094
moving forward in time here.

00:28:32.734 --> 00:28:36.641
Okay, but all of these
selections transformations and

00:28:36.642 --> 00:28:41.210
the selections are part of
a grammar of interaction.

00:28:41.211 --> 00:28:45.410
And there's no templates or
so whatsoever.

00:28:45.411 --> 00:28:48.254
And so, these are reusable
across a variety of different

00:28:48.255 --> 00:28:50.514
selection types,
all of these components.

00:28:52.332 --> 00:28:55.536
So here, for example, is a
continuous region selection, and

00:28:55.537 --> 00:28:57.580
it's called an interval.

00:28:57.581 --> 00:29:00.800
And now, by default, it allows
me to select a region in

00:29:00.801 --> 00:29:04.522
both the x and the y dimension,
and so it can create this brush.

00:29:07.323 --> 00:29:11.332
But I can go ahead and use this
transform to restrict it to

00:29:11.333 --> 00:29:16.040
a single dimension here, just
the dimension that's used for x.

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:21.724
And now,
we have a brush that can only

00:29:21.725 --> 00:29:25.131
be moved over horsepower.

00:29:25.132 --> 00:29:29.240
And then we can also use a bind
transform to bind the selection

00:29:29.241 --> 00:29:33.180
to the scale function,
rather than input element.

00:29:33.181 --> 00:29:35.504
And so, any guess what
happens if we do that?

00:29:37.506 --> 00:29:42.480
Binding selection to scale,
well this is what happens.

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:47.840
We can now start to pan and
zoom in the scatter plot, and

00:29:47.841 --> 00:29:49.080
this might feel
a little bit magical.

00:29:49.081 --> 00:29:52.516
But let me unpack what's
happening under the hood here.

00:29:55.454 --> 00:29:58.966
What's happening is that we're
establishing a two way binding

00:29:58.967 --> 00:30:01.580
between the selection and
the scales.

00:30:01.581 --> 00:30:05.277
So the selection is populated
by the scale domains, and then

00:30:05.278 --> 00:30:09.274
the scale domains themselves are
being driven by the selection.

00:30:09.275 --> 00:30:16.494
And so this is what gives us
this panning and zooming.

00:30:16.495 --> 00:30:19.469
So yeah,
there's this two-way binding and

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:21.910
then this reverse population.

00:30:21.911 --> 00:30:25.711
And as you can see, there's
implicitly, Vega-Lite creates

00:30:25.712 --> 00:30:30.260
this binding here, but you
don't have to manually do this.

00:30:30.261 --> 00:30:32.010
But you could, of course,
override it or

00:30:32.011 --> 00:30:34.560
put it into different
parts of a specification.

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:40.920
So all the interactions
I have shown you so

00:30:40.921 --> 00:30:45.250
far have been restricted
to one single view.

00:30:45.251 --> 00:30:47.760
But the reason why we designed
multi-view operators and

00:30:47.761 --> 00:30:51.080
the grammar of
interaction is because

00:30:51.081 --> 00:30:54.190
these interaction techniques
are most effective when we're

00:30:54.191 --> 00:30:56.850
coordinating multiple
distinct visualizations.

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:03.270
And so I can use a repeat
operator to go from a single

00:31:03.271 --> 00:31:04.140
scatter plot,

00:31:04.141 --> 00:31:07.820
like the one I was using before,
to a matrix of scatter plots.

00:31:07.821 --> 00:31:11.210
And now we can start brushing
and linking between all of them.

00:31:11.211 --> 00:31:12.460
And automatically,

00:31:12.461 --> 00:31:17.470
all of the selections work and
these force are views.

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:22.250
And with these sorts
of selections and

00:31:22.251 --> 00:31:25.234
transformations, a whole number
of other custom techniques fall

00:31:25.235 --> 00:31:26.580
out very naturally.

00:31:26.581 --> 00:31:31.280
I can set out an Overview+Detail
for Focus+Context interaction.

00:31:31.281 --> 00:31:32.890
Let me show you what
this might look like.

00:31:32.891 --> 00:31:36.360
So we start just an area chart.

00:31:36.361 --> 00:31:37.350
We have the same area,

00:31:37.351 --> 00:31:40.818
try it again,
just with slightly taller.

00:31:40.819 --> 00:31:42.970
And then, we're adding a,

00:31:42.971 --> 00:31:47.200
we can add a selection to the
upper chart, and this case, it's

00:31:47.201 --> 00:31:50.940
an interval selection restricted
to x, so we get the brush.

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:55.880
And then we can use that
same selection to change

00:31:55.881 --> 00:31:58.970
the scale domain of
the second chart.

00:31:58.971 --> 00:32:00.290
And then we get
this overview and

00:32:00.291 --> 00:32:04.500
detail, brooks and
[INAUDIBLE] entex visualization.

00:32:04.501 --> 00:32:07.650
This is similar to the other
example where I said

00:32:07.651 --> 00:32:08.780
some magic happened.

00:32:08.781 --> 00:32:12.160
Here, in this case, we split
where the selection is used and

00:32:12.161 --> 00:32:15.770
where the selection is defined
into two separate views.

00:32:15.771 --> 00:32:20.830
&gt;&gt; Is the word region your key
word or is it arbitrary text?

00:32:20.831 --> 00:32:21.630
&gt;&gt; It's arbitrary text.

00:32:21.631 --> 00:32:23.510
This is the name
of your selection.

00:32:23.511 --> 00:32:25.388
You can define multiple
selections here,

00:32:25.389 --> 00:32:26.177
if you wanted to.

00:32:30.197 --> 00:32:33.695
Okay, so as a final example
of what we can do with this

00:32:33.696 --> 00:32:36.558
interaction in the gram
of interactions,

00:32:36.559 --> 00:32:37.989
let me walk through,

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:42.060
walk you through how to create
a variant of cross filtering.

00:32:43.930 --> 00:32:44.750
Cross filtering, of course,

00:32:44.751 --> 00:32:47.030
being a very popular
visualization technique.

00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:51.400
So we will start with
a histogram, and

00:32:51.401 --> 00:32:54.180
in this case it's flight data.

00:32:54.181 --> 00:32:57.530
So this is a histogram that
shows us for the binned hour,

00:32:57.531 --> 00:33:00.840
how many flights are taking
off at that time.

00:33:02.730 --> 00:33:07.043
Then we might repeat the same
charts to look at different

00:33:07.044 --> 00:33:09.429
variables, so besides hour,

00:33:09.430 --> 00:33:14.600
also the delay and the distance,
how far the flight was.

00:33:14.601 --> 00:33:18.431
So we just replace the field
with the reference to

00:33:18.432 --> 00:33:19.464
the column.

00:33:19.465 --> 00:33:23.644
And now, what we want to do
is have this at some kind of

00:33:23.645 --> 00:33:25.935
layered interaction here.

00:33:25.936 --> 00:33:31.580
So first we add a second layer,
which is just the same as above.

00:33:31.581 --> 00:33:35.390
But we changed the color
to be this kind of gold.

00:33:35.391 --> 00:33:38.014
And now we have two times
the same charts on top of

00:33:38.015 --> 00:33:38.743
each other.

00:33:42.110 --> 00:33:45.440
So let me just focus a little
bit and collapse this down.

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:50.223
So now we can add
a selection to the chart,

00:33:50.224 --> 00:33:53.441
which is against just
restricted to x.

00:33:53.442 --> 00:33:56.264
So we have the brush and we can
start brushing in these charts,

00:33:56.265 --> 00:33:58.235
but nothing happens so far.

00:33:58.236 --> 00:34:02.820
But we can use the selection
then to filter the data in

00:34:02.821 --> 00:34:06.440
the other chart,
in the one with the golden bars.

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:13.410
And so now, What we get
is this cross filtering

00:34:13.411 --> 00:34:16.399
interaction where the golden
bars in the other

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:20.320
charts correspond to the data
that is selected by the brush.

00:34:23.290 --> 00:34:24.460
And this is all you need to

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:28.700
get cross filtering
working in Vega-Lite.

00:34:28.701 --> 00:34:31.380
The cool thing is, what we're
talking about here is 35 lines

00:34:31.381 --> 00:34:34.190
of JSON in a very
composable manner.

00:34:34.191 --> 00:34:36.974
So we might be able to just
extend this to add another chart

00:34:36.975 --> 00:34:38.313
that we can interact with.

00:34:40.697 --> 00:34:43.300
Okay, so now that you've
seen our language design and

00:34:43.301 --> 00:34:46.185
kinda what thoughts went into
the creation of this grammar of

00:34:46.186 --> 00:34:47.387
interactive graphics.

00:34:47.388 --> 00:34:49.549
Now we'll show you how
you can use Vega-Lite and

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:50.930
what we've built on top of it.

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:57.059
&gt;&gt; Cool, so first, let's see
how you can use Vega-Lite as

00:34:57.060 --> 00:34:58.430
a programming tool kit.

00:34:59.750 --> 00:35:02.520
So to use Vega-Lite we
provide a compiler that

00:35:02.521 --> 00:35:06.690
translates a Vega-Lite
specification into a low level

00:35:06.691 --> 00:35:09.840
specification that we
also developed at UDAP.

00:35:09.841 --> 00:35:11.980
You can then use
Vega's run time and

00:35:11.981 --> 00:35:14.980
create a visualization
both natively on the web.

00:35:14.981 --> 00:35:16.090
Or via a server and

00:35:16.091 --> 00:35:19.190
Windows visualization using
either Canvas or SVG.

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:25.737
And because we use the
underlying Vega architecture,

00:35:25.738 --> 00:35:29.370
we also support our
streaming data as well.

00:35:29.371 --> 00:35:33.659
And by using a declarative
JSON syntax we can still

00:35:33.660 --> 00:35:38.190
a file format for altering and
sharing visualization.

00:35:38.191 --> 00:35:41.120
And provide buying support for
different languages.

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:45.810
For example to perform
analysis in Python,

00:35:45.811 --> 00:35:48.030
you can use a library
called Altair,

00:35:48.031 --> 00:35:51.180
which is developed by Brian
Granger and Jake VanderPlas.

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:56.594
With Altair we can create
a histogram that we have

00:35:56.595 --> 00:36:00.532
built earlier in Vega-Lite
using a native Python API,

00:36:00.533 --> 00:36:04.480
that map there leads to
our visualizing text.

00:36:04.481 --> 00:36:08.820
This can be convenient if you
perform the analysis in Python.

00:36:08.821 --> 00:36:12.420
And we would like to also
highlight that Altair's API

00:36:12.421 --> 00:36:16.350
is automatically generated
from Vega-Lite JSON schema.

00:36:16.351 --> 00:36:21.145
This mean that when we publish
minor version, the Altair team

00:36:21.146 --> 00:36:25.210
mostly need to just that
script to update their API.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:29.418
And this approach not
native to just Python,

00:36:29.419 --> 00:36:33.139
there are other projects that
try to wrap Vega-Lite and

00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:34.893
other languages as well.

00:36:34.894 --> 00:36:36.771
In a recent article
by Dan Saber,

00:36:36.772 --> 00:36:40.529
which compared different Python
data visualization libraries,

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:44.420
Dan concludes that it's the
1:1:1 mapping between thinking,

00:36:44.421 --> 00:36:48.192
code and visualization that's
his favorite thing about Altair.

00:36:48.193 --> 00:36:52.680
And of course, the underlying
Vega-Lite language design.

00:36:52.681 --> 00:36:56.360
And the Altair team themselves
also very excited about

00:36:56.361 --> 00:36:56.920
our design.

00:36:56.921 --> 00:36:59.920
And mention that they
say Vega and Vega-Lite,

00:36:59.921 --> 00:37:02.640
perhaps the best
existing candidates for

00:37:02.641 --> 00:37:05.500
a principled lingua franca
of data visualization.

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:10.910
And just a few weeks ago,
we're excited that JupyterLab,

00:37:10.911 --> 00:37:13.970
which is the next
generation of the popular

00:37:13.971 --> 00:37:18.395
Jupyter Notebook Wood chips
weigh in Vega-Lite by default.

00:37:20.039 --> 00:37:24.056
In addition to use Vega-Lite
directly and via wrapper APIs,

00:37:24.057 --> 00:37:27.233
Vega-Lite can also enable
higher level tools.

00:37:27.234 --> 00:37:31.825
Like graphical user interface
applications that can let user

00:37:31.826 --> 00:37:37.390
create Vega-Lite plots via UI,
without writing code directly.

00:37:37.391 --> 00:37:39.740
And we can even use Vega-Lite
as a representation for

00:37:39.741 --> 00:37:41.330
visualization recommendation.

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:46.140
To give an example of a tool
that does both of these,

00:37:46.141 --> 00:37:50.700
we'd like to show you Voyager
visualization to augment manual

00:37:50.701 --> 00:37:53.480
visualization specification
with recommendation.

00:37:53.481 --> 00:37:58.060
And the goal for these two is
to promote better it's for

00:37:58.061 --> 00:38:02.304
the analysis and [INAUDIBLE] for
meta analysis [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:02.305 --> 00:38:04.360
And to the part this goal,

00:38:04.361 --> 00:38:08.288
we should use Vega-Lite to
[INAUDIBLE] specify and

00:38:08.289 --> 00:38:12.709
use Vega-Lite as a model
to recommend visualizaiton.

00:38:12.710 --> 00:38:15.357
Here's the virtual interface,
and

00:38:15.358 --> 00:38:18.440
here we are showing
a dataset about cards.

00:38:18.441 --> 00:38:23.337
And Voyager is a single unified
tool that allow user to

00:38:23.338 --> 00:38:27.084
with decision and also show
recommendation to user.

00:38:27.085 --> 00:38:30.010
After loading the dataset
without having to create any

00:38:30.011 --> 00:38:31.540
views manually.

00:38:31.541 --> 00:38:34.620
We first show univariate
summaries, so

00:38:34.621 --> 00:38:37.570
that users can browse
different chart.

00:38:37.571 --> 00:38:40.220
And say that let's

00:38:40.221 --> 00:38:42.770
see a summary of different
data fields for example.

00:38:42.771 --> 00:38:47.160
Here you can see that in this
data set a lot of cars have

00:38:47.161 --> 00:38:48.850
an even number of cylinders.

00:38:48.851 --> 00:38:53.300
And at least some type of car
have odd number of cylinders.

00:38:53.301 --> 00:38:56.200
You can also see that this
data set is quite old,

00:38:56.201 --> 00:39:01.386
it's from 1970 to 80 something,
older than me at least.

00:39:01.387 --> 00:39:06.810
[LAUGH] And, you can also use
a drag and drop interface

00:39:06.811 --> 00:39:11.500
to be your specific view
similar to Tableau.

00:39:11.501 --> 00:39:17.087
For example, dragging
Horsepower to the x shelf would

00:39:17.088 --> 00:39:23.180
create a visualization
that shows of Horsepower.

00:39:23.181 --> 00:39:27.958
And under the field, we'll just
create Vega-Lite specification

00:39:27.959 --> 00:39:30.890
that map Horsepower
to the external, and

00:39:30.891 --> 00:39:34.190
use Vega-Lite to render
the visualization.

00:39:34.191 --> 00:39:36.022
Based on this specified view,

00:39:36.023 --> 00:39:39.404
we'll just also suggest
different time related views to

00:39:39.405 --> 00:39:42.298
help analysts convert
into variables.

00:39:42.299 --> 00:39:45.410
And alternative ways to
summarize or encode the data.

00:39:45.411 --> 00:39:46.080
For example,

00:39:46.081 --> 00:39:49.510
up here we recommend
the related summary views.

00:39:49.511 --> 00:39:54.140
And to do so, once you consider
the Vega-Lite classification

00:39:54.141 --> 00:39:55.980
of the data above.

00:39:55.981 --> 00:39:58.440
And determine if you can

00:39:58.441 --> 00:40:02.470
calculate summary views by
applying aggregation of binning.

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:07.670
And here, we see a part that
shows a histogram of horsepower

00:40:07.671 --> 00:40:10.380
and a plot that shows
mean of horsepower.

00:40:12.330 --> 00:40:14.835
You can similarly suggest
other kinds of plot sets.

00:40:14.836 --> 00:40:19.260
For example, suggesting a plot
that shows horsepower with other

00:40:19.261 --> 00:40:23.365
additional variable, like
horsepower and acceleration,

00:40:23.366 --> 00:40:27.474
horsepower and displacement,
or horsepower and.

00:40:30.463 --> 00:40:34.475
And to give user more control
to create a collection of

00:40:34.476 --> 00:40:39.201
views manually, provide
a wildcard to specify wiring

00:40:39.202 --> 00:40:43.190
properties of
the visualization collection.

00:40:43.191 --> 00:40:46.910
For example, on the left
we have wildcards for

00:40:46.911 --> 00:40:48.670
wiring multiple fields.

00:40:48.671 --> 00:40:51.769
If I drag a quantitative
fields y bar to y,

00:40:51.770 --> 00:40:56.202
we can create a gallery of
scatter plots showing horsepower

00:40:56.203 --> 00:40:59.050
with other quantitative
variables.

00:41:04.365 --> 00:41:08.012
Under the hood,
we also use Vega-Lite for

00:41:08.013 --> 00:41:10.350
[INAUDIBLE] use a wildcard.

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:16.576
And in this case, for a
wildcard, we basically enumerate

00:41:16.577 --> 00:41:20.719
different qualitative fields for
the y channel.

00:41:22.158 --> 00:41:24.761
With all these different
interactions, data plots.

00:41:24.762 --> 00:41:28.156
And this can rapidly
explore different aspect of

00:41:28.157 --> 00:41:29.396
the data set, and

00:41:29.397 --> 00:41:34.050
create specific visualizations
to address that specific goals.

00:41:34.051 --> 00:41:38.573
And to cap again, whenever you
serve specified things manually,

00:41:38.574 --> 00:41:41.886
Voyager creates a Vega-Lite
specification and

00:41:41.887 --> 00:41:43.190
renders the plot.

00:41:43.191 --> 00:41:47.512
And also analyze the Intend
the user provide from the visual

00:41:47.513 --> 00:41:51.670
interface, and provide it
with fully recommendation.

00:41:53.050 --> 00:41:57.157
For more details about different
kind of recommendation and

00:41:57.158 --> 00:42:01.051
specifications, as we'll ask
the underlying curly language,

00:42:01.052 --> 00:42:03.829
that we view on top
of what I call home.

00:42:03.830 --> 00:42:06.070
You can look at our research
paper, and I was so

00:42:06.071 --> 00:42:08.947
happy to come to give another
talk about Voyager in detail,

00:42:08.948 --> 00:42:10.170
if you're interested.

00:42:12.048 --> 00:42:15.342
So from Voyager, we have see
that Vega-Lite can be used to

00:42:15.343 --> 00:42:19.180
support recommendations of
data and visual encodings.

00:42:19.181 --> 00:42:21.650
One area of visual work that
we are excited about is,

00:42:21.651 --> 00:42:24.349
applying similar approach to
recommended interactions.

00:42:25.890 --> 00:42:29.190
We legalize concise in tasks
to composing interaction.

00:42:29.191 --> 00:42:32.440
We can just add one small
more target that changes

00:42:32.441 --> 00:42:33.740
to a specification, and

00:42:33.741 --> 00:42:37.260
generate atomically different
interaction technique.

00:42:37.261 --> 00:42:40.810
With these small changes,
we can easily write a program to

00:42:40.811 --> 00:42:43.850
enumerate this space of
interaction design, for

00:42:43.851 --> 00:42:46.840
visualization and recommend
interaction techniques as well.

00:42:49.217 --> 00:42:50.712
To summarize in this talk,

00:42:50.713 --> 00:42:53.117
we have shown you
the design of Vega-Lite,

00:42:53.118 --> 00:42:56.560
which include building blocks
for specifying single views.

00:42:56.561 --> 00:42:59.580
And compose them into
interactive multi-view graphics.

00:42:59.581 --> 00:43:02.470
We have also shown that
Vega-Lite can be used directly

00:43:02.471 --> 00:43:04.970
as a programming tool,
but very and

00:43:04.971 --> 00:43:09.298
on the web and
we are other wrappers such as.

00:43:09.299 --> 00:43:13.560
In addition, Vega-Lite can
enable graphical user interface,

00:43:13.561 --> 00:43:16.280
as well as recommendation
in a system like Voyager.

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:21.230
And we are happy to
announce that last Tuesday,

00:43:21.231 --> 00:43:25.080
we released the first production
version of Vega-Lite 2,

00:43:25.081 --> 00:43:27.630
which improved all
the composition and

00:43:27.631 --> 00:43:29.700
interaction features we
described in this talk.

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:33.190
And we also have tutorials, and

00:43:33.191 --> 00:43:38.810
documentations on line at
vega.github.io/vega-lite.

00:43:38.811 --> 00:43:42.930
And if you're interested, you
can start paying with Vega-Lite,

00:43:42.931 --> 00:43:46.780
using our online editor, without
the need to install anything,

00:43:46.781 --> 00:43:50.200
we also provide auto completion,
and some hints for

00:43:50.201 --> 00:43:52.840
documentation on the editor.

00:43:52.841 --> 00:43:56.910
And finally, we'd like to thank
